-- allium: 3
-- live-index.allium
--
-- Scope: in-memory staging of transactions before they are flushed into a block.
-- Includes: the live index and its tables, row operations, the transaction lifecycle, and snapshot visibility.
-- Excludes: block storage and trie serialisation (db.allium), leader election (log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
--
-- Concurrency model:
--   One writer at a time. Every transaction operation below runs serially, whichever role owns the live index.
--   Many concurrent readers. A snapshot can be held and read while writes continue.
--   Writes never block reads: a snapshot is frozen at creation, and stays valid until its last holder releases it.
--
-- Pipelining — resolve, append, read back, apply:
--   A LEADER applies its own writes to the durable live tables only once it has read them BACK off its own replica log, at its own term.
--   That read-back is the fence — Raft's ReadIndex. The four stages:
--
--     1. RESOLVE. A transaction arriving from the source log or an external source is resolved against what the leader already holds, then STAGED onto the resolver's queue.
--        Staging advances the resolver's RESOLVED head, so later transactions in the same stream resolve against it (read-your-writes).
--        The transaction is not yet visible to external queries.
--     2. APPEND. The staged transaction is written to the replica log, STAMPED with the leader's term.
--        Control messages — block boundaries, term claims, tries added and deleted — are appended the same way, and stamped the same way.
--     3. READ BACK. The leader reads its own replica log from the position its promotion claimed.
--     4. APPLY. Each record has met the term fence on the way in, so what reaches the term is its own to apply (see ApplyRecord).
--        Applying the leader's own transaction imports it from the QUEUE HEAD, which still holds its rows, so nothing is re-read from the replica record — that record's job was to confirm durability and term.
--        THIS is where a committed transaction becomes queryable, and where an external caller's durability handle completes.
--
--   Resolution and apply never interleave, so a transaction is never half-applied.
--   Applying does not refresh the shared snapshot: that is a lazily-advanced cache, rebuilt on the next demanding read (see RefreshSharedSnapshot).
--
--   A FOLLOWER has no resolver and no read-back: the messages it imports are already durable, so it advances the durable head directly.
--
--   Block cut — resolve-side gauge, then PAUSE:
--   The block boundary is cut off a RESOLVE-side row gauge, seeded from the rows already applied into the open block, so a new leader inheriting a partially-filled block cuts it where the old leader would have — else block sizes drift across restarts.
--   It is cut AHEAD of the applied count, which lags.
--   When the gauge crosses rows_per_block the resolver injects a block boundary, in resolution order so that it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's, and PAUSES resolution until that block is adopted.
--   The pause carries two obligations.
--   It keeps the buffer of messages held behind an open block from overflowing, no transaction interleaving between the boundary and its upload.
--   And it keeps anything from applying between the point where the live tables are written to block storage and the point where they are cleared — rows applied there would be written nowhere.
--   The pause is deliberately the status quo; block double-buffering is separate future work (#4495).
--
--   Two heads, two homes — callers choose:
--     - the RESOLVED head, on the resolver: the highest transaction RESOLVED, and so staged.
--       Advanced at STAGE. Drives resolution — the next tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
--     - the DURABLE head, on the live index: the highest transaction APPLIED, i.e. read back and imported.
--       Advanced at APPLY. The query basis.
--   Resolved LEADS; durable LAGS it by exactly everything staged but not yet applied.
--   ("Applied" throughout this file means the far end — imported into the durable live index — never merely staged.)
--
--   The queue belongs to the RESOLVER rather than to the live index, and that is forced by read-your-writes: a transaction resolving now must see every predecessor not yet applied, so whoever resolves has to hold the staged transactions.
--   The live index stays the durable layer, and stays resolver-agnostic.
--
--   Per-transaction durability handles:
--   Each staged EXTERNAL-SOURCE transaction carries a handle its caller may await; source-log transactions carry none, the source log being the durability record for those.
--   The handle completes on APPLY — durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.
--   Every path on which the transaction will never be applied MUST fail the handle instead, or a caller waits forever: a failure before the transaction is staged fails it where it happens, and a transaction still staged when the resolver ends is failed there (see ResolverEnds).
--
-- Critical invariant: external queries see only durably-replicated transactions — those the leader has read BACK off its own replica log and applied.
-- A query returned to a user must never include a transaction that could vanish if the leader crashed before its replica-log write landed, nor one a newer-term leader superseded.
-- Resolution sees durable ⊕ staged ⊕ the transaction's own writes.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity TransactionKey {
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
}

external entity TableRef {
    dbName: String
    schemaName: String
    tableName: String
}

external entity IID {}   -- 16-byte entity identifier

external entity Document {}   -- the actual row data for puts

external entity MemorySegment {}
    -- A frozen slice of one table's rows, paired with the trie indexing its IID column.
    -- The slice is taken when the snapshot is created and is unaffected by later writes to the table.
    -- See: memory-hash-trie.allium.

external entity TrieCatalogSnap {}
    -- Frozen per-table trie-catalog state: which tries exist on disk, and the highest L0 block index per table.

external entity TableCatalogSnap {}
    -- Frozen per-table metadata for everything already flushed: each table's column types and its count of rows ever written.
    -- The historical half of a column's declared type.

external entity DurabilityHandle {}
    -- A per-transaction handle a caller may await, completed once the transaction has been read back and imported into the durable live tables.
    -- Carried by external-source transactions only; source-log transactions carry none.
    -- Completed on apply (ApplyResolvedTx), or FAILED on any path where the transaction will never be applied — see the header.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value ValidTimeRange {
    from: Timestamp
    to: Timestamp
}

value Row {
    iid: IID
    system_from: Timestamp
    valid_time: ValidTimeRange
    op: Op
}

value Op {
    -- Exactly one leg is populated per row.
    -- Modelled as three optionals rather than a sum type only because of what this file's language version can express; "exactly one populated" is the rule.
    put: Put?
    delete: Delete?
    erase: Erase?
}

value Put {
    doc: Document
}

value Delete { }   -- null marker in the "delete" leg
value Erase { }    -- null marker in the "erase" leg

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity LiveIndex {
    -- DURABLE head / QUERY BASIS: the highest transaction APPLIED into the durable live tables.
    -- External queries open at it.
    -- On a leader it is advanced by ApplyResolvedTx on read-back, so it LAGS the RESOLVED head by everything staged but not yet applied; on a follower every imported message is already durable, so there is no lag.
    --
    -- The RESOLVED head, which drives resolution, is NOT here: on a leader it lives on the resolver.
    -- The live index knows only the durable layer, and callers choose which head to read.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    rows_per_block: Integer

    -- Rows already applied into the currently-open block.
    -- Seeds the resolver's block gauge when a leader takes the role, so a leader inheriting a partially-filled block cuts it where the previous one would have.
    block_row_count: Integer

    -- Durable live tables: they hold only applied rows.
    tables: LiveTable with index = this

    -- The current shared external snapshot: a lazily-advanced cache, rebuilt only when a reader demands a basis it does not satisfy.
    -- Apply and NextBlock leave it stale. Its basis is the durable head as of the last rebuild.
    shared_snap: Snapshot

    row_count: tables.sum(t => t.row_count)
    is_full: row_count >= rows_per_block
}

entity TxResolver {
    -- The leader's transaction resolver: it turns a source-log message, a database attach or detach, or an external source's writer into a resolved transaction, and holds the queue of resolved-but-not-yet-applied transactions until the leader has applied them.
    --
    -- The queue lives HERE rather than beside it because of READ-YOUR-WRITES: a transaction resolving now must see every predecessor still queued, so whoever resolves has to hold the staged transactions.
    -- Resolution and the queue are one concern, not two.
    --
    -- It exists for the length of one leader term. Seeded at the durable head when the term takes the role; absent on a follower, which only imports already-durable messages.
    -- The durable live index it pipelines into. The link is one-way: the resolver names the index, and the index stays resolver-agnostic.
    index: LiveIndex

    -- RESOLVED head: the highest transaction RESOLVED, and so staged — not the highest applied.
    -- Drives RESOLUTION: the next external-source tx_id is one past it, system-time smoothing reads it, and resolution layers read-your-writes over the staged transactions.
    -- Seeded from the durable head, then advanced at STAGE rather than at apply, so it LEADS the durable head by exactly the transactions staged but not yet applied.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    -- The staged transactions, oldest first (resolution order).
    -- Stage appends; ApplyResolvedTx removes the head after importing it.
    staged: StagedTx with resolver = this

    is_empty: staged.count = 0
}

entity StagedTx {
    -- One resolved transaction held in the resolver's queue: no longer open, so it can neither be written to nor queried, but retaining its per-table rows.
    -- Those rows are what ApplyResolvedTx imports on read-back, and what later transactions resolving behind it read (read-your-writes).
    --
    -- It carries no replica-log position: its durability is confirmed when the leader reads its record back at its own term.
    resolver: TxResolver
    tx_key: TransactionKey

    -- The caller's durability handle, for an external-source transaction; null for a source-log one.
    durability: DurabilityHandle?

    tables: StagedTxTable with staged_tx = this
}

entity StagedTxTable {
    -- One transaction's rows for one table, imported into the matching durable LiveTable when the transaction is applied.
    staged_tx: StagedTx
    table_ref: TableRef
    rows: Set<Row>

    row_count: rows.count
}

entity LiveTable {
    -- Live tables are removed by NextBlock, not just emptied.
    index: LiveIndex
    table_ref: TableRef
    rows: Set<Row>

    row_count: rows.count
}

entity OpenTx {
    index: LiveIndex

    -- The resolver this transaction resolves against.
    -- A resolving transaction reads durable ⊕ the resolver's staged transactions ⊕ its own writes, so it carries the resolver.
    -- Always present: open transactions exist only on a leader, where the resolver exists.
    resolver: TxResolver

    tx_key: TransactionKey
    status: active | committed | aborted

    transitions status {
        active -> committed
        active -> aborted
        terminal: committed, aborted
    }

    table_txs: LiveTableTx with transaction = this

    system_from: tx_key.system_time
}

entity LiveTableTx {
    transaction: OpenTx
    table_ref: TableRef
    live_table: LiveTable?   -- null if the table is new
    is_new_table: live_table = null

    rows: Set<Row>           -- uncommitted rows in this transaction
    row_count: rows.count
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Snapshots (visibility)
--
-- A Snapshot is a point-in-time view over one database.
-- It is composed of per-table TableSnapshots, each holding a frozen segment of that table's rows.
-- Opened from within an active transaction, a TableSnapshot also layers the staged transactions' rows and then the transaction's own, so a query inside the transaction sees durable rows, its predecessors' rows and its own.
--
-- A Snapshot also pins the two catalogs a reader needs alongside those segments: the trie catalog, which locates the historical tries on disk, and the table catalog's per-table metadata.
-- Pinning them is what stops a reader combining pinned data with catalog state read live at the moment it happens to ask.
--
-- The three captures are NOT atomic with respect to one another.
-- They are taken in order — trie catalog, then segments, then table catalog — so a block landing concurrently can fall between them.
-- The table catalog is taken last deliberately: a type view newer than the data view over-approximates, which is always safe, whereas an older one would declare a narrower type than the rows being scanned, which is not.
--
-- That matters for a column's declared type, which is the join of what the table catalog knows (flushed blocks), what the segments know (unflushed), and what the superseded live tables know.
-- The column set and the types resolving it come from one capture, or they can disagree.
--
-- L0 supersession:
--   After a block is flushed its rows exist twice — in the live table that produced them, and in the L0 trie written from it.
--   A snapshot therefore excludes any live table the trie catalog's L0 watermark has reached, so those rows are read once, from the L0.
--
--   Their column TYPES are retained regardless.
--   A block becomes visible in two steps: the L0 reaches the trie catalog first, the block's column types reach the table catalog second.
--   A snapshot taken between the two would otherwise have no source for those types at all — declaring a narrower type than the rows it is about to scan, and failing to resolve a brand-new table entirely.
--   Retaining the types bridges that gap, and is safe because the join is idempotent: once the catalog catches up the answer is unchanged, whatever order the writers ran in.
--
--   The live table outlives the gap in both directions — it is cleared only after the table catalog has been updated — so a source is always available while the gap is open.
--
-- Consistency guarantees:
--   - A snapshot's segments are frozen at creation and do not observe later writes.
--   - Its captured catalogs are likewise frozen.
--   - Those captures are ordered, not simultaneous: a concurrently-landing block can fall between them, which is what "L0 supersession" above covers.
--   - Commits are atomic: all of a transaction's rows become visible together, or none do.
--   - Multiple snapshots can be held concurrently without interference.
--   - Writes don't block reads: the shared external snapshot is rebuilt lazily, and an existing snapshot stays valid until released.
--
-- Sharing:
--   External snapshots are shared — the live index publishes a single current one, and each external reader holds it until it closes it.
--   A snapshot's segments survive exactly as long as its last holder.
--   Transaction snapshots are per-transaction and are not shared.
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Snapshot {
    basis_tx: TransactionKey?

    table_snapshots: TableSnapshot with snapshot = this

    -- Frozen catalog state, captured with the segments above.
    trie_catalog_snap: TrieCatalogSnap
    table_catalog_snap: TableCatalogSnap

    -- Tables the pinned table catalog knows about: everything already flushed, plus the data-backed system tables seeded when the database opens.
    -- A table here resolves with no segment at all.
    catalog_tables: Set<TableRef>

    -- Live tables kept out of table_snapshots because a published L0 already covers their rows, but whose column types are still needed until the table catalog records them.
    -- See "L0 supersession" above.
    --
    -- Supersession applies to the DURABLE live table only, so this does not partition against segment_tables: a superseded table that the resolving transaction or a staged one also writes to appears in both, and contributes through both.
    superseded_tables: Set<TableRef>

    -- The tables carrying a segment in this snapshot.
    segment_tables: table_snapshots -> table_ref

    -- Derived: which tables resolve in this snapshot.
    -- A table can resolve without a segment — via the catalog, or via supersession — so the segments alone do not answer it.
    visible_tables: catalog_tables + superseded_tables + segment_tables
}

entity TableSnapshot {
    snapshot: Snapshot
    table_ref: TableRef

    -- The DURABLE live rows for this table. Absent when the table has no durable data at snapshot time.
    -- This is the only layer on an external snapshot.
    live_segment: MemorySegment?

    -- The staged transactions' rows for this table, layered on the durable rows, oldest first — one per staged transaction that touched it.
    -- A just-staged transaction's rows live ONLY here until they are applied.
    -- Empty on an external snapshot, which sees durable data only; populated on a transaction snapshot so that a resolving transaction reads its staged predecessors.
    staged_segments: Set<MemorySegment>

    -- The enclosing transaction's own uncommitted rows, layered on top of the staged ones.
    -- Present only on a transaction snapshot where the transaction has written to this table.
    tx_segment: MemorySegment?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The block-sizing bound. The resolver's block gauge crosses this to cut a boundary.
    rows_per_block: Integer = 102_400
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

default ValidTimeRange erase_valid_time = { from: MIN_TIMESTAMP, to: MAX_TIMESTAMP }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Transaction lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

rule StartTransaction {
    -- Started by the resolver, which hands itself to the transaction so it can resolve against durable ⊕ staged (read-your-writes).
    when: StartTransaction(index, resolver, tx_key)

    requires: resolver.index = index

    ensures: OpenTx.created(
        index: index,
        resolver: resolver,
        tx_key: tx_key,
        status: active
    )
}

rule GetTableTx {
    -- Idempotent: returns the existing LiveTableTx where one already exists for this transaction and table.
    when: GetTableTx(tx, table_ref)

    requires: tx.status = active

    let existing_table_tx = tx.table_txs.find(tt => tt.table_ref = table_ref)

    ensures:
        if existing_table_tx != null:
            existing_table_tx
        else:
            let existing_table = tx.index.tables.find(t => t.table_ref = table_ref)
            LiveTableTx.created(
                transaction: tx,
                table_ref: table_ref,
                live_table: existing_table,
                rows: {}
            )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Row operations
------------------------------------------------------------

rule LogPut {
    when: LogPut(table_tx, iid, valid_time, doc)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: valid_time,
        op: Op{ put: Put{ doc: doc } }
    })
}

rule LogDelete {
    when: LogDelete(table_tx, iid, valid_time)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: valid_time,
        op: Op{ delete: Delete{} }
    })
}

rule LogErase {
    when: LogErase(table_tx, iid)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: erase_valid_time,
        op: Op{ erase: Erase{} }
    })
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Commit and abort (RESOLVE side — stage and append, not apply)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CommitTransaction {
    -- The resolver STAGES a committed transaction onto its queue and advances its RESOLVED head.
    -- The staged transaction is then appended to the replica log.
    --
    -- It does NOT advance the DURABLE head and does NOT refresh the external shared snapshot.
    -- Those happen only when the leader reads the transaction BACK off its own replica log and imports it (ApplyResolvedTx).
    -- Later transactions in the same stream resolve against the queue (read-your-writes), but external queries cannot yet see it.
    --
    -- durability is the caller's handle for an EXTERNAL-SOURCE transaction, and absent for a source-log one.
    when: CommitTransaction(tx, durability?)

    requires: tx.status = active

    let resolver = tx.resolver

    ensures: tx.status = committed
    ensures: resolver.latest_completed_tx = tx.tx_key

    -- Stage the transaction at the tail, in resolution order, retaining its per-table rows.
    -- Tables the transaction wrote no rows to are skipped.
    ensures:
        let staged_tx = StagedTx.created(
            resolver: resolver,
            tx_key: tx.tx_key,
            durability: durability
        )
        for table_tx in tx.table_txs where table_tx.rows.count > 0:
            StagedTxTable.created(
                staged_tx: staged_tx,
                table_ref: table_tx.table_ref,
                rows: table_tx.rows
            )
}

rule AbortTransaction {
    when: AbortTransaction(tx)

    requires: tx.status = active

    ensures: tx.status = aborted
    -- The RESOLVED head tracks "resolved up to", not "successfully committed up to": an aborted transaction consumes a tx_id and advances the head, so the next tx_id is correct.
    -- It is staged and appended like any other, so it too is read back and applied.
    ensures: tx.resolver.latest_completed_tx = tx.tx_key
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Read-back and apply (where a transaction becomes durable)
--
-- The leader applies its own writes only after reading them back off its own replica log, at its own term.
-- The term on read-back is the fence.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ApplyRecord {
    -- Apply one record read back off the leader's OWN replica log.
    -- The record is this term's own: terms are unique per leader, and the fence has settled the record's term before the term is offered it, discarding anything the log has moved past and ending this term on anything above it.
    -- So the apply holds no term comparison of its own.
    --
    -- A record ABOVE this term has superseded it, and the term ends there rather than here — EXPECTED, so it does not poison the watchers, and every transaction still staged on it fails its handle (see ResolverEnds).
    --
    -- The fence and its high-water are db.allium's, because they span the database on this node rather than one leader's term.
    when: ApplyRecord(resolver, record)

    ensures:
        RecordApplied(resolver: resolver)
}

rule ApplyResolvedTx {
    -- The leader reads its OWN staged transaction back at its own term and IMPORTS it.
    -- This is where a committed transaction becomes queryable: durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.
    --
    -- It imports from the QUEUE HEAD, which still holds the transaction's rows, so nothing is re-read from the replica record.
    -- The head must match the transaction just read back; a mismatch is a fault.
    --
    -- The DURABLE head advance ALONE makes the transaction queryable.
    -- The shared external snapshot is a lazily-advanced cache, rebuilt on the next demanding read, and is not refreshed here.
    when: RecordApplied(resolver)

    requires: not resolver.is_empty

    let index = resolver.index
    let staged_tx = resolver.staged.first

    ensures:
        -- Import this transaction's rows into the (possibly new) durable live tables.
        for staged_table in staged_tx.tables:
            let live_table =
                let existing = index.tables.find(t => t.table_ref = staged_table.table_ref)
                if existing != null:
                    existing
                else:
                    LiveTable.created(
                        index: index,
                        table_ref: staged_table.table_ref,
                        rows: {}
                    )
            live_table.rows.addAll(staged_table.rows)

        -- Advance the DURABLE head. The RESOLVED head passed this transaction at stage time.
        index.latest_completed_tx = staged_tx.tx_key

        -- Complete an external caller's handle now that the transaction is durable, and last of all, so the caller unblocks after the transaction is queryable rather than before.
        if staged_tx.durability != null:
            HandleCompleted(staged_tx.durability)

        -- Drop the head, AFTER importing it.
        not exists staged_tx
}

rule ResolverEnds {
    -- A leader term ends — superseded, faulted or demoted — and its resolver with it.
    -- Every transaction still staged will never be applied, so each one's durability handle is FAILED with the cause, and no caller waits forever.
    -- Source-log transactions carry no handle and contribute nothing.
    --
    -- The staged transactions themselves are DISCARDED. They were never visible to an external query, so discarding them costs nothing: the next leader re-reads them from the source log and re-resolves them.
    -- The durable live index is untouched, and still reflects a complete prefix of the replica log.
    when: ResolverExited(resolver, cause)

    ensures:
        for staged_tx in resolver.staged where staged_tx.durability != null:
            HandleFailed(staged_tx.durability, cause)

        for staged_tx in resolver.staged:
            not exists staged_tx

        not exists resolver
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Snapshot visibility
--
-- CRITICAL: external queries see only DURABLY-REPLICATED transactions — durable live rows only, nothing staged.
-- Resolution — a query inside an in-flight transaction — sees durable ⊕ staged ⊕ the transaction's own writes.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule OpenExternalSnapshot {
    -- Called by queries outside any transaction.
    -- min_basis is the caller's minimum read basis, and may be absent.
    --
    -- The index holds a single shared snapshot, whose basis is the durable head as of its last rebuild and whose rows are the DURABLE live rows only.
    -- It is a lazily-advanced cache: where the caller has no minimum basis, or the cache already satisfies it, the cache is returned as it stands; otherwise it is rebuilt first.
    -- The durable basis guarantees the head is at or past min_basis by the time this runs, so a rebuild can always reach a satisfying basis.
    -- The caller holds the snapshot until it closes it.
    when: OpenSnapshot(index, min_basis?)

    ensures:
        -- Rebuild only when the cache is too stale for the requested basis.
        if min_basis != null and index.shared_snap.basis_tx.system_time < min_basis:
            RefreshSharedSnapshot(index)
        index.shared_snap
}

rule OpenTransactionSnapshot {
    -- Called by queries within an in-flight resolving transaction, e.g. UPDATE ... WHERE.
    -- Only an active transaction can open one, and a transaction snapshot is never shared.
    --
    -- A resolving transaction must read all prior transactions whether DURABLE or merely STAGED, plus its own uncommitted writes.
    -- So each TableSnapshot layers, bottom to top: the durable rows, the staged transactions' rows oldest first, and the transaction's own.
    when: OpenSnapshot(tx)

    requires: tx.status = active

    ensures:
        let snap = Snapshot.created(basis_tx: tx.tx_key)
        let resolver = tx.resolver

        -- One TableSnapshot per table the transaction touched.
        for table_tx in tx.table_txs:
            let live_seg = if table_tx.live_table != null: MemorySegment.for(table_tx.live_table) else: null
            let tx_seg = if table_tx.rows.count > 0: MemorySegment.for(table_tx) else: null
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: snap,
                table_ref: table_tx.table_ref,
                live_segment: live_seg,
                staged_segments: StagedSegments.for(resolver, table_tx.table_ref),
                tx_segment: tx_seg
            )

        -- Plus the remaining live tables it hasn't touched: durable rows and any staged rows, no transaction rows.
        for table in tx.index.tables
                where not tx.table_txs.any(tt => tt.table_ref = table.table_ref):
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: snap,
                table_ref: table.table_ref,
                live_segment: MemorySegment.for(table),
                staged_segments: StagedSegments.for(resolver, table.table_ref),
                tx_segment: null
            )

        snap
}

rule RefreshSharedSnapshot {
    -- The rebuild primitive for the shared external snapshot.
    -- NOT triggered by apply or NextBlock — the cache is advanced lazily.
    -- It is invoked by OpenExternalSnapshot where the cache is too stale for the requested basis, and out of band by compaction, which changes the trie catalog without advancing the durable head.
    -- Readers already holding the previous shared snapshot keep it, and it stays valid for as long as they do.
    when: RefreshSharedSnapshot(index)

    ensures:
        let new_snap = Snapshot.created(
            -- DURABLE basis: an external snapshot sees only durably-replicated transactions, never the RESOLVED head.
            basis_tx: index.latest_completed_tx
        )
        for table in index.tables:
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: new_snap,
                table_ref: table.table_ref,
                live_segment: MemorySegment.for(table),
                -- External snapshot: durable data only, no staged layers.
                staged_segments: {},
                tx_segment: null
            )
        index.shared_snap = new_snap
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Block lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CutBlock {
    -- The RESOLVER cuts a block off its resolve-side row gauge: when the gauge crosses rows_per_block after staging a transaction, it injects a boundary and PAUSES resolution.
    -- The boundary is injected in resolution order, so it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's.
    -- Resolution stays paused until the block is adopted, so the buffer of messages held behind the open block cannot overflow.
    -- The gauge resets. See the header "Block cut".
    when: CutBlock(index, block_idx, latest_processed_msg_id)

    ensures: BlockBoundaryAppended(index, block_idx, latest_processed_msg_id)
}

rule FinishBlock {
    -- Triggered when the boundary is read back: by the leader at its own term, or by a follower reading a durable one.
    -- By log order the live index now holds exactly this block's transactions, so its durable contents are written to block storage.
    -- Rows remain in memory until NextBlock.
    --
    -- Because resolution paused at the boundary and stays paused until the block is adopted, nothing was staged and applied in between — so a durable L0 block never contains rows from a later block.
    when: FinishBlock(index, block_idx)

    ensures: BlockWritten(index, block_idx)
}

rule NextBlock {
    when: NextBlock(index)

    -- Remove all live tables; their data is now in block storage.
    -- Tables are recreated on demand by future transactions.
    ensures: index.tables = {}

    -- The shared snapshot is NOT refreshed here.
    -- A subsequent reader whose basis the now-stale cache still satisfies may reuse it: its segments are frozen, independent of the cleared tables, and remain valid.
    -- A reader demanding a newer basis rebuilds the cache.
}
